{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Do not defile yourselves in any of these things.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim): whether in all of them or in some of them. Meaning, even in a partial [manner], as though it said ״do not defile yourselves in one of all these.״ Now one needs to know why the verse spoke in this order, for it should have said ״do not defile yourselves in one of all these,״ so that there be no room for error. Further, it is difficult that the verse concludes ״for in all these the nations were defiled,״ and concerning the nations we have no basis to say they were defiled only in one, for one swept away in licentiousness has his hand in everything. Moreover, the plain sense of ״in all these they were defiled״ indicates that they were defiled in all of them — and if so, what reason would we hear in the matter being commanded that the intention is ״do not defile yourselves in all of them״?" "<b>It appears</b> to explain with understanding, according to the true matter, why HaShem did not reveal in the Torah the gradations of the commandments and their inner property (segulah). Indeed King Solomon, peace be upon him, came and said (Proverbs 5:6): ״אֹרַח חַיִּים פֶּן תְּפַלֵּס״ — and our Rabbis of blessed memory (Avot 2:1) followed after him and said that we must not say this commandment is great and that one is small, for you do not know, etc. And they proved this (Deuteronomy Rabbah ch. 6; Jerusalem Pe'ah 1:1) from the fact that HaShem fixed equal reward for the greatest of the great together with the smallest of the small. Yet over all this I submit that reason dictates there are indeed commandments that are greater by far — so why did HaShem conceal the matter?" "<b>And it appears</b> that since the minor commandments are needed just as the major ones — for each and every commandment has its own inner property (segulah), and what difference does it make if its reward is small, given that its reward is nonetheless something unattainable as the reward of a great commandment — and all the commandments are needed for the rectification of the soul, therefore HaShem commanded minor and major on one level, so that Israel would perform them all equally. This is the saying of Solomon (Ecclesiastes 9:10): ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר תִּמְצָא יָדְךָ לַעֲשׂוֹת בְּכֹחֲךָ עֲשֵׂה.״" "<b>Further,</b> another reason appears: since there are commandments whose inner property (segulah) is to lengthen days in this world, and some whose property is wealth, and some whose property is children, and so on along these lines — HaShem was concerned that if the matter were revealed, each person would go and exert himself in what he desires and in what he needs, and would not exert himself in the other commandments as necessary. Therefore He concealed the matter. And through this a person will exert himself in all of them, so that there may also come into his hand a commandment whose property is something he needs. And through this there is a reason for what we see — that several people who are wicked toward Heaven and toward their fellow men nonetheless have their households full of peace and filled with every good out of sheer abundance — for it is possible that such a person happened to fulfill a commandment whose property is success in this world, and the transgressions he commits do not prevent the commandment's property and its success. And all this applies in the category of positive commandments; but regarding negative commandments, the matter is as follows: there are transgressions whose punishment is in the World to Come, and some whose punishment is suffering over a long span of time, and some whose punishment is [dealt out] in mercy, and some with utter cruelty. And do you not find several wicked people who transgressed [sins punishable by] karet and were not cut off, even though they persisted in the power of wickedness until the time of cutting-off had passed? Rather, it is certain that there are [sins] for which one is paid in this world and there are [sins] for which one is paid in the World to Come. And it is a known matter that fear more powerfully impresses the hearts of men than does reproof that is paid in this world, because it is visible, more than the hidden punishment." "<b>Now,</b> it has preceded us that HaShem, blessed be He, is exacting even upon His creatures who are near to Him, lest they speak a word that is not along the line of righteousness — and even things that appear as falsehood. Go forth and learn from the statement of Rabbi Akiva to his companions (Ḥagigah 14b): when you arrive at the marble stones of pure quality, do not say ״water, water.״ And from here we draw an inference to ״choice silver — the tongue of the righteous״ — He is HaShem our Elohim, the Source of truth, for He insists that nothing be written in the Torah except things that shine brilliantly in the attribute of truth." "<b>And with this</b> we come to the explanation, which is that since this punishment of the land vomiting out its inhabitants applies only to one particular sin among the abominations mentioned in the pericope, as I shall explain nearby, whereas the other abominations have a different punishment, as El Ḥai Tamim De'im (the living God, perfect in knowledge) judged according to the nature of each abomination — therefore, when He came to command His people, He wished to inform them that among the abominations concerning which He had warned them there is one particular abomination that would cause the land to vomit them out, and HaShem did not wish to reveal which one, so that out of fear they would guard themselves from all of them together, as I have written, for the revealed punishment terrifies more than the hidden one. And this is the measure of the matter: <b>Do not defile yourselves in any of these</b> — meaning, in any one of all these. <b>For in all these the nations were defiled, which I am casting out before you, and the land was defiled, and I visited its iniquity upon it</b> — meaning, one of them is the cause, and that one I visited upon the land, and it vomited out its inhabitants. From this you learn that for one sin the land vomited them out. If so, you are compelled to say that the statement <b>Do not defile yourselves in any of these</b> means: in any one of all these, since the punishment was for one, and you do not know which it is." "He further said: <b>And you shall keep… and you shall not do any of the abominations… for all… they did.</b> The verse intends by this to inform them that the people of the land committed all the abominations, and there is not a single one among all the abominations that they did not do. And the purpose of this is so that they all enter the category of the suspected thing, for whatever the people of the land did not do carries no fear of the land's vomiting. Therefore it was necessary to say ״for all…״ etc. This matter is like a vessel of water in which a snake vomited: all vessels that enter the category of doubt regarding this vessel — a person trembles at approaching them, and so too is this matter. And even though the sin that causes the vomiting is one, while the abominations He commands are many, this matter has a fixed principle: a person is presumed to spread himself to commit abomination, and the presumption works against him and not in his favor, as I elaborated on this in my work on Yoreh De'ah (Peri To'ar, siman 110). Since this is so, everything is fixed as a fifty-fifty split. And after He instilled fear in them through the revealed punishment, He returned and said: <b>For whoever shall do</b> etc. — meaning, that for all the other abominations as well there is a severe punishment of excision of the soul; only, one of them causes the revealed punishment."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :register {:flags {5 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:58:38.213572565Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}